--TEST--
an in-process request to a path the compiled table does not claim still runs the program, which decides for itself what a miss means — `rule:testing/in-process-request`, `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The boundary of the sibling case: there, the table claimed the path and the
// match crossed; here it claims nothing and the request is answered anyway.
// `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler` is why -- the match is a computation and never a dispatch, so a
// `null` route is a fact the program reads rather than a `404` anything sends.
// A door that refused a miss would refuse every request of a program declaring
// no `#[Route]` at all, which is `rule:routing/table-is-opt-in`'s opt-in rule inverted.
//
// The `if` branch is the child, for the reason the sibling case's comment
// states in full: `Core\Env::context()` is `Request` in the run that answers.
class Api {
    #[Core\Route(path: "/claimed", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function claimed(): string { return "claimed"; }
}

if (Core\Env::context() == Core\Env::Context::Request) {
    var $matched = Core\Request::route();
    if ($matched == null) {
        // The program's own answer to a miss, chosen here and by nobody else.
        Core\Response::setStatus(404);
        echo "the table claimed nothing";
    } else {
        echo "unexpectedly matched";
    }
} else {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/nothing/here");
    echo "status ", $answered->status(), "\n";
    echo "body ", $answered->body(), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
status 404
body the table claimed nothing
